Also accept the sign-extended form of ioctl requests with the direction bit set.

The kernel truncates the request to unsigned int, but the seccomp filter sees
the raw register. musl passes the request as a signed int, so every _IOR/_IOWR
request arrives as 0xffffffff........ and fails the equality test, killing the
device processes with SIGSYS. Spell out both forms.

Only the x86_64 policies are patched; the build targets x86_64 only.

diff --git a/jail/seccomp/x86_64/common_device.policy b/jail/seccomp/x86_64/common_device.policy
index ac4d3e9..7e85f3b 100644
--- a/jail/seccomp/x86_64/common_device.policy
+++ b/jail/seccomp/x86_64/common_device.policy
@@ -75,4 +75,6 @@ uname: 1
 ## Rules for vmm-swap
 userfaultfd: 1
 # 0xc018aa3f == UFFDIO_API, 0xaa00 == USERFAULTFD_IOC_NEW
-ioctl: arg1 == 0xc018aa3f || arg1 == 0xaa00
+# Requests with the direction bit set are matched twice: musl passes the
+# request as a signed int, so the filter sees it sign-extended.
+ioctl: arg1 == 0xc018aa3f || arg1 == 0xffffffffc018aa3f || arg1 == 0xaa00
